course: dictionary
lesson: 7381
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. Plug can mean a part you push into a socket, a stopper for a bath or sink, or a verb meaning to block or to connect. Read the words around it to decide which one fits.
explain: «Pull the plug to let the water drain» is about the bath. The plug is the round stopper in the hole, because draining happens there. Swap in your meaning and check: the plug is the stopper, and the water can run out.
explain: «Mia panicked and pulled the plug in the middle of a save.» Here the words are not about a bath or a socket. Pulling the plug means stopping something. Try it: Mia stopped the game in the middle of a save. That makes sense, so this is the right meaning.
fact: A plug is the part on the end of an electric wire that you push into a socket to get power.
fact: A plug can also be a round rubber or plastic stopper that blocks the hole in a bath or sink.
fact: To plug a hole means to block it. To plug something in means to connect it to the electricity supply.
fact: «Pull the plug» can mean stop something, as in «pulled the plug in the middle of a save».
fact: Safety first: never touch plugs or sockets with wet hands, and tell a grown-up at once if a plug is hot or sparks.
exercise: In «Mia pulled the plug in the middle of a save», what does pulled the plug mean? | *To stop something | A round stopper for a sink | A socket in the wall | A wire with a lid
exercise: «Never touch a plug with wet hands.» What is a plug in this sentence? | To block a hole | A kind of cloth for cleaning | A round stopper for a bath | *The part on the end of an electric wire
exercise: «Plug the gap with a cloth.» What does plug mean here? | *To block it | To pull it out | To paint it | To make it louder
test: Leo says «Please plug in the kettle.» What does plug in mean? | *To connect it to the electricity supply | To pull the kettle apart | To block the hole in the kettle | To stop the kettle for good
test: Which is the safest thing to do if a plug feels hot or sparks? | Poke a fork into the socket to check | Pull it out quickly by the cord | Put a wet cloth on it and keep using it | *Tell a grown-up at once and do not touch it
exam: Why should you pull a plug out by holding the plug itself, not the cord? | *Yanking the cord can damage the wires and break their plastic cover | Holding the plug makes the fan go faster | The cord is always hot | Yanking the cord makes the plug bigger
